#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128781 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e290e8b2-7766-55ff-95da-99a3430b18e3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of an Inverse Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "b22d04e0-8c6a-507a-9d76-ab05adb32b10" :question/math-academy-id "e-11517" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w) \\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))$. The inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to(u(x,y,z), v(x,y,z), w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$.\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x>0$, $y \\ne 0$.*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$, denoted\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y, z))/(∂(u, v, w)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given $\\mathbf T^{-1}$. So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)}$, the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T^{-1}$.\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ using the relation\n$\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} = \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} \\right)^{-1}$.\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nu(x,y,z) = x^2, \\qquad v(x,y,z) = xy, \\qquad w(x,y,z) = yz\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} &= 2x & \\frac{∂u}{∂y}|= 0 & \\frac{∂u}{∂z}|= 0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x}|= y & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}|= x & \\frac{∂v}{∂z}|= 0 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x}|= 0 & \\frac{∂w}{∂y}|= z & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}|= y\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(u, v, w))/(∂(x, y, z)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 0 & 0 \\\\ y & x & 0 \\\\ 0 & z & y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2x\\begin{vmatrix}x & 0 \\\\ z & y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2x(xy - 0) \\\\\n&= 2x^{2}y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)}\n$$\n\nin terms of $u$, $v$, and $w$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2}y &= 2 \\cdot x \\cdot (xy) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{u} \\cdot v \\\\\n&= 2v\\sqrt{u}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y, z))/(∂(u, v, w)) &= ((∂(u, v, w))/(∂(x, y, z)))^{-1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2v\\sqrt{u}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128782 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b4a31c6-01be-5b15-88f5-9150f350e944" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "b16845a2-5f66-524a-90ea-37f8a8186f8f" :question/math-academy-id "e-11518" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$, where $\\mathbf T$ is defined as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$, denoted\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y, z))/(∂(u, v, w)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nx(u,v,w) =w-u^3, \\qquad y(u,v,w) = 2uv, \\qquad z(u,v,w) = u^2+v\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} &= -3u^{2} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v}|= 0 & \\frac{∂x}{∂w}|= 1 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u}|= 2v & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}|= 2u & \\frac{∂y}{∂w}|= 0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u}|= 2u & \\frac{∂z}{∂v}|= 1 & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}|= 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y, z))/(∂(u, v, w)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}-3u^{2} & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2v & 2u & 0 \\\\ 2u & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=-3u^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+ 1\\begin{vmatrix}2v & 2u \\\\ 2u & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=-3u^{2}(0 - 0) + 1(2v - 4u^{2}) \\\\\n&= 2(v - 2u^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} =0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y, z))/(∂(u, v, w)) &= 0 \\\\\n2(v - 2u^{2}) &= 0 \\\\\nv - 2u^{2} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v-2u^2 = 0$ defines the parabolic cylinder $v=2u^2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128783 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d433063f-f145-5bf4-8bd4-8d5e5b36b3d8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of a Three-Dimensional Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "66d757d2-f2ad-5f0d-b4f1-8d426f4c8351" :question/math-academy-id "e-11579" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that maps the Cartesian $uvw$-space onto the Cartesian $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n$x = 2u-v,\\qquad y = u+3v+w,\\qquad z = u+2v-w$." :question/worked-solution "For a transformation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$, denoted\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y, z))/(∂(u, v, w)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} &= 2 & \\frac{∂x}{∂v}|=-1 & \\frac{∂x}{∂w}|= 0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u}|= 1 & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}|= 3 & \\frac{∂y}{∂w}|= 1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u}|= 1 & \\frac{∂z}{∂v}|= 2 & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}|=-1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(∂(x, y, z))/(∂(u, v, w)) &= \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}- (-1) \\cdot\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2(-3 - 2) + (-1 - 1) \\\\\n&=-10 - 2 \\\\\n&=-12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e290e8b2-7766-55ff-95da-99a3430b18e3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of an Inverse Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "edd1b8fb-a982-5388-a199-722a82b95b93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294568} {:answer/id #uuid "06c836e0-229b-580d-a602-e7e8c5e81ea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294569} {:answer/id #uuid "fc94578a-29bd-50bd-865f-8a07e4caba95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294570} {:answer/id #uuid "3a303d05-743b-5ed4-889e-6ba1e4dcc85e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294571} {:answer/id #uuid "4495adaa-78ff-5e82-90ca-fa8312422455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4495adaa-78ff-5e82-90ca-fa8312422455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294572} :answer-field/id #uuid "d096282b-f341-56d8-b0da-efc1326e9112" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5372554f-c783-5b2a-8ab6-cd073e081874" :question/math-academy-id "q-161798" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=z,\\,w=4y-2{z}^{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =4 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =-4z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4z\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4z\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(0-4) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b4a31c6-01be-5b15-88f5-9150f350e944" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9ef2524-c316-5f5c-bb17-9410d6df7855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u = 2v$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126573} {:answer/id #uuid "af21f9b2-947b-5b4b-9b83-b2324aabe6c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $u =-w^{2}$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126574} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ef1406-0abe-5cfe-bc04-7ae04a77ecf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u = w$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126575} {:answer/id #uuid "e90dd075-4fb2-51bb-9367-44b42c15619d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw -$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126576} {:answer/id #uuid "0cffeeef-f100-51b5-ac9e-aa76adbf7807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $u = 2w^{2}$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af21f9b2-947b-5b4b-9b83-b2324aabe6c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $u =-w^{2}$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126574} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d271abc-b1d9-5284-9062-68834149c800" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126572}] :question/id #uuid "685e989d-8721-5a8c-a4d8-0e44efe4bc9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161819" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T$, where $T$ is defined as\n\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u^{2} - w^{2} \\\\ we^{u} \\\\ u - v\\end{bmatrix}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129665 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e290e8b2-7766-55ff-95da-99a3430b18e3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of an Inverse Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87d932d5-f4b9-58c7-a540-eab1c3639817" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{w}u^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127785} {:answer/id #uuid "c16660a0-766a-58fd-940c-73a14a37eb9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2u^{2}\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127786} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf020bc-ed02-5978-bf7c-b1c3a979b0ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2u^{2}\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127787} {:answer/id #uuid "37a6ce5c-5a9a-5e19-b56d-a0e63d2a2418" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{u^{2}}{2\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127788} {:answer/id #uuid "552ce6a8-0995-57c6-b1f4-c543f2867686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{u^{2}}{2\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c16660a0-766a-58fd-940c-73a14a37eb9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2u^{2}\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127786} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ba9a2e9-7024-556c-a598-30b64d5dc305" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127784}] :question/id #uuid "f85af4c0-4520-5614-952b-cb9d6d06c6c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-39300" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T: (u, v, w) → (x(u, v, w), y(u, v, w), z(u, v, w))$. The inverse transformation $T^{-1}(x, y, z) → (u(x, y, z), v(x, y, z), w(x, y, z))$ is given by\n\n$T^{-1}: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\sqrt{y} + z \\\\ y^{2}\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T$.\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x \\ne 0$ and $y > 0$.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129687 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d433063f-f145-5bf4-8bd4-8d5e5b36b3d8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of a Three-Dimensional Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b968aa55-e291-5a1f-926c-97f7a44d4d34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4vw" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127917} {:answer/id #uuid "0c1341bd-a102-5669-a16d-791369af1503" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127918} {:answer/id #uuid "52add2b0-6d74-5a18-b1e6-4162d66d956d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2vw\\cos (vw)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127919} {:answer/id #uuid "75409427-80be-5a98-9736-cc2d2093545b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4vw\\cos (vw)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127920} {:answer/id #uuid "7a7807c9-66d6-5c74-9ff4-d80d2acdae1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c1341bd-a102-5669-a16d-791369af1503" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127918} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b16f1bc-2636-5241-8175-a7ec56e20e07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127916}] :question/id #uuid "045d5f30-3ffc-5bb0-bb67-dd9e010fc71b" :question/math-academy-id "q-39666" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T$, defined as\n\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}4v \\\\ 3u^{2} + 2\\cos (vw) \\\\ vw\\end{bmatrix}$."}]}
